{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"rg -n '\"'^#|TODO|placeholder|stub|TASK'\"\\\\.md|exit-interview\\\\.json|/tmp|http|\"'`[''^`]+/[''^`]+`'\"' PLAN.md\"","aggregated_output":"1:# Director’s plan\n3:## 1. Build mandate and ownership\n15:## 2. Research foundation\n17:Research the original before implementation, including a direct look and listen where accessible. Keep a short evidence ledger in `docs/research.md`: source URL, what it confirms, what is interpretati [... omitted end of long line]\n19:### Confirmed source identity\n21:The [original rules booklet](https://odyssey2.info/library/manuals/us_kcmunchkin/K.C.%20Munchkin%20(USA).pdf) documents a controllable Munchkin, three Munchers, twelve moving Munchies, four-direction  [... omitted end of long line]\n23:A [contemporary first-hand description of the original audiovisual display](https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/F2/672/607/331150/) describes eight ordinary and four powered moving c [... omitted end of long line]\n27:An [original-system gameplay recording](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EznzG5HpmuM) and an [archived commercial](https://www.odyssey2.info/media/videos/video.php?id=8) are additional leads for the bu [... omitted end of long line]\n29:### Translation into this game\n42:## 3. The game the player receives\n56:### The first minute\n62:## 4. Authoritative rules and game feel\n64:Write a short rules specification in `docs/rules.md` before feature expansion. The implementation, bots, content validators, editor and tests must use the same rules.\n66:### Movement and topology\n74:### Twelve living targets\n84:### Three predators\n100:### Gates, tunnels and disappearing walls\n108:### Lives, scoring and completion\n116:## 5. Content and pacing\n133:Each stage definition contains its map, spawn points, food identities/routes or routing policy, tunnel pairs, gate specification, reveal rules, predator parameters, introduction hint and clear cue. Us [... omitted end of long line]\n137:## 6. Visual direction and animation\n154:## 7. Controller-first screens and input\n174:## 8. Engine foundation and implementation boundaries\n178:Read the [handoff README](https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/blob/8ecc2f62353761187bdfe226f327b7ea5d2eb691/dist/handoff/README.md), [API card](https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/blob [... omitted end of long line]\n184:### Verified integration points\n189:| Grid movement | `new E.GridMover({ cx, cy, speed })`, `update(dt, grid, direction, { keep: true })`, `place(cx, cy)`. Speed is cells/second; smooth `x/y` are cell coordinates, rendered at `(value +  [... omitted end of long line]\n193:| Screens | Extend `E.Game` menus/settings, `E.Menu` and the TV-scaled `E.Shell`; retain native focus, hover, rebinding and device prompts. |\n199:### Suggested project structure\n226:## 9. Procedural audio direction\n236:## 10. Multiplayer and AI Hub\n238:Research baseline: Hub Protocol v1, session client **1.4.0**, commit `35a1047f2067b2af3de3943f0fe452f39105d869`. Read the pinned [protocol](https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/blob/35a1047f2067b2 [... omitted end of long line]\n240:### Explicit scope\n246:### Required lifecycle\n255:8. On `end`, stop accepting gameplay input, advancing gameplay, sending messages and producing sound. Hub owns the results/removal flow. Fatal load/unsupported-browser failures use `AIHub.fail` rather [... omitted end of long line]\n259:### Transport and authority\n271:## 11. Maze workshop and expansion tools\n279:Workshop boards use the campaign’s schema and simulation. Seeded generation uses the same validator. Save a few varied, known-good templates. Test edit → validate → save → reload → export → import → p [... omitted end of long line]\n281:## 12. Attract mode and developer tools\n283:### Attract demonstration\n289:### Developer mode\n295:Use these tools to balance the actual game. Do not tune solely by reading numeric tables. Record major tuning outcomes in `docs/balance.md` so the final defaults have an explainable basis.\n297:## 13. Headless verification and artifact packaging\n299:### One quiet command\n301:Expose a single development-folder command, such as **`node tools/test.mjs`**, covering simulation checks, browser checks, content validation and Hub conformance. It prints failures and one summary li [... omitted end of long line]\n305:### Essential automated coverage\n315:| Controls/screens | Keyboard, mouse focus/click, stubbed standard pad, two local seats, last-device prompts, glyph option, reconnect, touch multitasking/cancel, small-screen and TV layout. |\n325:### Browser and Hub execution\n327:Prefer engine headless tools and `file://` for standalone. Use the installed Playwright **1.63.0** environment or a matching local package; do not reinstall browsers. Chromium is the primary automated [... omitted end of long line]\n329:Use device profiles for phone/tablet checks and a `navigator.getGamepads` stub in a browser init script for physical-controller behavior. These are stand-ins; do not claim tests on actual controllers, [... omitted end of long line]\n331:Keep Hub harness dependencies together under `tools/hub/` and serve the harness and built game from a common allowed origin. If a server is required, bind **127.0.0.1** with port **0**, read the OS-se [... omitted end of long line]\n333:Run the official harness for declared 1–2 seats, including one-player coverage and two-player normal, `latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5`, host-leave and dedicated-server cases. The harness supports `game= [... omitted end of long line]\n337:### Limited visual inspection\n341:### Single-file build\n349:## 14. Production sequence and exit gates\n365:When a check fails, correct the underlying rule, data or integration before broadening scope. No user-facing placeholder, inert menu item, fake slider, decorative network status or stub editor counts  [... omitted end of long line]\n367:## 15. Release information and acceptance\n385:- No TODOs, placeholders, stubbed features, stray kit screens or unused promises remain in the delivered experience.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\np = Path('planner-exit-interview.json')\ndata = json.loads(p.read_text())\nexpected = [\n    'What did you like most about this prompt?',\n    'What was confusing?',\n    'What was the hardest thing to do?',\n    'What would you change in this prompt?',\n]\nassert isinstance(data, list) and len(data) == 4\nassert [entry['question'] for entry in data] == expected\nassert all(set(entry) == {'question', 'answer'} and isinstance(entry['answer'], str) and entry['answer'].strip() for entry in data)\nplan = Path('PLAN.md').read_text()\nbrief = Path('TASK.md').read_text()\nassert '20261106-021155' not in plan\nassert '[GameTitle' not in plan\nassert '/home/' not in plan\nassert '../' not in plan\nassert '1.05M' in plan and 'gpt-6.1-sol ultracode' in plan\nassert 'fifty' in plan and 'random number generator' in plan\nassert '\\\\x00' not in plan\nprint(f'PASS: planner interview has all 4 exact questions and nonempty answers; plan has {len(plan.split()):,} words and no run-specific output paths.')\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
